  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 302 AM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nThe extended periods start off dry Thu night but that will be short \nlived. There will be a developing wave across the central Gulf \nmoving northward and beginning to push tropical moisture into the \nFL/AL coast by Fri morning. At the same time there is a developing \nfrontal boundary moving slowly SE out of the Mid to lower MS river \nvalley approaching NW GA by daybreak Sat. This tropical wave pushes \ndeep level moisture northward across the Gulf coast states (TX, LA, \nMS, AL, FL) Friday and begins to interact with the southern end of \nthe frontal boundary across LA and MS. We will begin to see some \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms Friday but our best chances will \nbe Sat and Sun as the frontal boundary sinks south across the state \nand directing the tropical moisture across North and Central GA. \nRight now not seeing a lot of instability with this set up \nFri/Sat/Sun but with temps expected in the 80s Fri and Sat and the \nfront moving in to act as a lifting mechanism we will see some \nthunderstorms. Looks like our biggest concern may be the amount of \nprecipitation we could see through the beginning of next week. \n\nThe aforementioned frontal boundary kind of stalls across N GA Sun \nthen begins pushing south Mon into Tue. with this front acting as a \nguide for the deep tropical moisture to stream across the area for 3 \nalmost 4 days. Current QPF totals showing 3\" to 5\" across north GA \nFri to Tues with 2\" to 4\" across central portions of the state for \nthe same timeframe. If this amount of precipitation falls in just a \nfew days we could be dealing with some stream/river flooding and \nsome flash flooding if storms begin to train across the same\nareas.",
